    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `skip`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `skip` function is used to skip the execution of a test or a module during testing. It raises a `Skipped` exception with a specified reason, which signals to the test runner that the test should be skipped. The `reason` parameter is a string that provides a message to the user explaining why the test is being skipped. The `allow_module_level` parameter, when set to `True`, allows the function to be called at the module level, which will stop the execution of the module and prevent the collection of any tests defined in that module. The function does not return any value; instead, it raises an exception. It relies on the `_with_exception` decorator to handle the exception raising process. The `__tracebackhide__` attribute is set to `True` to hide the traceback for the `Skipped` exception, making the output cleaner. This function is useful for dynamically skipping tests based on runtime conditions, but it is generally recommended to use the `pytest.mark.skipif` marker for static conditions.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger